R = {
'2-1-1': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: you are on purpose, and you are loved because God made you, not because of what you do. At seven this steadies kids who are starting to compare themselves with classmates.',
 'Man and woman are made in God’s image (CCC 355). Each person is body and soul together, and God creates each soul directly; it does not die with the body (CCC 362, 366). Animals have life but not a spiritual soul.',
 'Say it simply: “You can think, choose and love, like God does. A dog can’t pray or say sorry.” If asked, “Do pets go to heaven?”, say: “We know God loves what he made; the Church doesn’t tell us more, so we trust him.”',
 'Watch for children who feel unlike others: disability, body image, or being teased. Say every body is good and every person is in God’s image. Avoid “body is bad, soul is good”; the body is part of who we are.',
 'Quick check: ask, “What can you do that a puppy can’t?” Listen for think, choose, love, pray. Ask, “Who is made in God’s image?” and look for “everybody, even people who are different from me.”',
]),
'2-1-2': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: God lets you really choose, and good choices make you stronger inside. At seven, children start making choices when no adult is watching, so this is the right time to name it.',
 'Freedom is the power to act deliberately and take responsibility for it (CCC 1731). The more we do good, the freer we become; choosing evil is an abuse of freedom (CCC 1733).',
 'Say: “Free means free to love. A fish is most free in water.” To “Why did God let him choose wrong?”, answer: “Love only counts if it is chosen. God doesn’t make robots; he wants friends.”',
 'Avoid framing choices as only “be good or God is mad.” Be careful with children whose choices are heavily controlled at home or who feel blamed for others’ behavior. Teach that mistakes can be repaired.',
 'Quick check: give a two-option scene, such as a kid who finds a dropped toy. Ask which choice helps him grow in goodness and why. Listen for “I choose, and it makes me better,” not just “I’d get in trouble.”',
]),
'2-1-3': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: when we do wrong, the first word is mercy. This prepares children for Reconciliation, so they meet it as running to the Father, not facing a judge.',
 'Sin is an offense against God that turns our hearts from his love (CCC 1850); it also wounds our neighbor. Jesus came as the revelation of God’s mercy to sinners (CCC 1846). God does not stop loving us.',
 'Say: “Sin is like a crack in a friendship. It’s our doing, and God wants to mend it.” To “Is God mad at me?”, say: “He is sad when we hurt people, but he runs to us like the father in the story.”',
 'Do not call a child bad; say the choice was bad. Kids with harsh homes may hear “father” as scary, so stress the father in Luke 15. Do not make a child confess a home situation.',
 'Quick check: ask, “In the story, what did the father do when he saw his son far off?” Look for “ran, hugged, threw a party.” Then ask, “What does that tell you about God?”',
]),
'2-2-1': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: Jesus knows you by name and will not leave you. For children who worry about being lost, left out or alone, this is the heart of trust.',
 'Jesus calls himself the Good Shepherd who lays down his life for the sheep (John 10:11). The Church is his flock, and he is the one gate (CCC 754). He leads us to the Father.',
 'Say: “A shepherd knows each sheep. Jesus knows you better than anybody.” To “Then why did something bad happen?”, say: “Jesus is sad too and stays with us; we may not understand why, and we can ask him.”',
 'Take care with children who have lost a parent or live in unstable homes: do not promise nothing bad will happen. Promise Jesus is with them. Make clear that Jesus is the shepherd, not a sheep.',
 'Quick check: ask, “What does a good shepherd do for his sheep?” Listen for know, protect, find. Then, “When you feel scared, what can you tell Jesus?” A child answering in the first person got it.',
]),
'2-2-2': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: Jesus uses ordinary stories to show what God is like and how his kingdom grows. Children this age love stories and can handle a little picture-meaning.',
 'Jesus teaches the kingdom by parables, inviting people to a radical choice (CCC 546). The kingdom is God’s reign, already begun and growing (CCC 543). Mustard seed: small start, big growth (Matthew 13:31-32).',
 'Say: “The kingdom is when God is the boss of hearts, and it starts as small as a seed.” To “Where is the kingdom?”, say: “It’s wherever people let God lead; it’s not on a map, and it will be complete in heaven.”',
 'Pitfall: turning each parable into a moral rule and losing the surprise. Let the story do the work. Do not tell children the lost sheep means some people are “bad sheep”; the point is that every one is wanted.',
 'Quick check: have a child retell the lost sheep in their own words, then ask, “Who is the shepherd? Who is the sheep?” They should say God finds each one and is glad.',
]),
'2-2-3': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: Jesus shows a happiness bigger than getting stuff. At seven, children measure joy by toys and treats, so this offers a better picture of being blessed.',
 'The Beatitudes are at the heart of Jesus’ preaching and respond to our desire for happiness, which God placed in us (CCC 1716-1718). They describe the way to be with God, not a list of rules.',
 'Say: “Blessed means deeply happy, with God’s joy. The kind, the peacemakers, the ones who share are close to God.” To “Why are the poor and sad happy?”, say: “God is close to them and comforts them.”',
 'Do not suggest that suffering is good, or that poor or grieving children should be glad about it. Do not turn them into a grading chart. Be careful with children who face real loss.',
 'Quick check: ask each child to pick one Beatitude and tell what it looks like at recess. Anyone who shows a concrete kind or peacemaking act has understood.',
]),
'2-3-1': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: God gave you an inner guide for right and wrong, and it gets better when you train it. This sets up examination of conscience and Reconciliation.',
 'Conscience is a judgment of reason that recognizes the moral quality of an act (CCC 1778). It must be informed, and a well-formed conscience is upright and truthful (CCC 1783). We must always follow a sure judgment.',
 'Say: “Your conscience is like an inner coach. You help it learn by listening to your parents, the Church and the Bible.” To “How do I know it’s not just me?”, say: “If it points to what is good, kind and true, trust it and ask a grown-up.”',
 'Do not conflate conscience with guilt feelings or fear. Some children, scrupulous or anxious, feel guilty about everything. Reassure that a conscience guides us; it doesn’t keep scolding.',
 'Quick check: give a situation, such as a friend asking you to copy homework. Ask the child to use three questions: Is it kind? Is it true? Is it safe? Listen for whether they apply them.',
]),
'2-3-2': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: looking honestly at our hearts is a calm, short, hopeful step toward God’s mercy. For children preparing for First Reconciliation, this removes dread about “not remembering”.',
 'Examination of conscience prepares for the sacrament in the light of God’s Word (CCC 1454). Confession itself frees us and helps reconciliation (CCC 1455). Psalm 139 asks God to help us see our hearts.',
 'Say: “Ask God to show you, like switching on a light, then think of a few times you didn’t love well.” To “What if I forget one?”, say: “Tell what you remember. God knows your heart, and his mercy is bigger than your memory.”',
 'Avoid long lists or guilt prompts, and do not ask children to name private family matters. Scrupulous children need short questions and a firm ending in mercy. Never use examination to shame.',
 'Quick check: ask each child to tell you, in order, how to begin (pray), what to ask (three or four questions), and how it ends (God’s mercy). Look for confidence, not fear.',
]),
'2-3-3': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: Reconciliation is Jesus forgiving you through the priest, and it brings peace. Children should leave the lesson less afraid and knowing what to say and do.',
 'The priest greets and blesses, the word of God is read, the penitent confesses and is given a penance, and the priest absolves in Christ’s name (CCC 1450, 1455, 1480). Jesus gave this power to the apostles (John 20:21-23).',
 'Say: “You tell Jesus your sins and the priest listens in Jesus’ place. Then he says the words that forgive you.” To “Will the priest tell anyone?”, say: “Never. The seal means a priest can never tell (CCC 1467).”',
 'Never imply perfect wording is required; help gently with words. Plan for children who feel unsafe with adults: make sure they know a trusted adult is nearby and that they can ask for help. Practice only in a reassuring, play-acting way.',
 'Quick check: do a rehearsal with a doll or helper, and have the child lead the steps in order. A child who finishes with “Thanks be to God” and smiles is ready.',
]),
'2-4-1': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: at the Last Supper Jesus gave us himself in the Eucharist so he could stay with us. For children preparing for First Communion, Mass should feel like his gift, not a lecture.',
 'At the Last Supper Jesus instituted the Eucharist, giving his Body and Blood under bread and wine and telling the apostles to do this in his memory (CCC 1337, 1339-1340). Mass makes present his one sacrifice.',
 'Say: “Jesus knew he would die and rise, so he gave us a way to keep him close. Each Mass we do what he did.” To “Is Jesus dying again at Mass?”, say: “No, the Mass makes his one gift on the Cross present for us.”',
 'Don’t let it become “just a meal” or a past event. Do not drop in big words like transubstantiation. Respect that some children come from homes where Mass is rare; welcome them warmly.',
 'Quick check: ask, “What did Jesus say about the bread? What did he tell his friends to do?” Look for “This is my body” and “Do this in memory of me.”',
]),
'2-4-2': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: in the Eucharist it is truly Jesus, not a symbol. This is the heart of First Communion preparation and children can grasp it with trust more than explanation.',
 'Jesus is present in the Eucharist in a unique way: Body, Blood, soul and divinity, the whole Christ (CCC 1374-1376). The bread and wine are changed by the priest’s words and the Holy Spirit; it still looks like bread.',
 'Say: “It looks like bread, but Jesus said it is his Body, and we believe him.” To “Why does it still taste like bread?”, say: “God changed what it is, not what we see. It’s a mystery we trust.”',
 'Avoid saying “like” Jesus, which makes it a symbol. Avoid saying Jesus is only present at Communion; he remains in the tabernacle. Don’t treat reception as a test; children who cannot receive yet need kindness.',
 'Quick check: ask, “What is in the host after the priest prays?” Look for “Jesus.” Then ask how we show respect (quiet, bow, fold hands). Reverent behavior shows understanding.',
]),
'2-4-3': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: Mass has a shape, and children can take part in every part. Knowing what comes next lets a seven-year-old feel like a participant, not a bystander.',
 'The Mass has two great tables, the Word and the Eucharist, framed by the gathering and the sending forth (CCC 1346, 1348-1355). Emmaus shows the same pattern: Jesus explains Scripture, then breaks the bread.',
 'Say: “We come together, listen to God, receive Jesus, and then go out to share him.” To “Why do we go every week?”, say: “Friends meet often. Mass is where we meet Jesus and his family.”',
 'Pitfall: boredom blamed on the child. Give jobs: sing, respond, look, listen for one word. Be kind to families with restless or special-needs children; don’t praise only quiet ones.',
 'Quick check: shuffle four picture cards (gathering, word, Eucharist, sending) and have the child put them in order and name one response said at that part.',
]),
'2-5-1': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: Mary’s “yes” shows how to say yes to God and that she is our mother who leads us to Jesus. Children respond naturally to a loving mother figure.',
 'God chose Mary freely to be mother of the Son, and she freely consented (CCC 488, 494). Her motherhood in the order of grace continues for us (CCC 969). At Cana she says, “Do whatever he tells you.”',
 'Say: “Mary said yes even when it was scary, and she always points us to Jesus.” To “Is Mary God?”, say: “No, she is a human being and God’s chosen mother. We honor her; we worship God alone.”',
 'Avoid presenting Mary as a competitor to Jesus or as an answer to every need. Be sensitive to children with absent mothers or from non-Catholic homes who may find Marian devotion new.',
 'Quick check: ask, “What did Mary say to the angel? What did she say to the servants at Cana?” Look for “Let it be done to me” and “Do whatever he tells you.” Then, “Who does Mary lead us to?”',
]),
'2-5-2': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: saints are real people who loved God in ordinary life, and you can too. This counters the idea that holiness is only for adults or the special.',
 'The Church canonizes people who lived heroic virtue, showing the power of the Spirit and giving hope (CCC 828). The saints are our examples, friends and intercessors; holiness is for everyone (CCC 2030).',
 'Say: “A saint is someone who is now with God and who loved him well on earth.” To “Are saints dead?”, say: “Their bodies died but they are alive with God, and they pray for us.”',
 'Don’t only choose saints who were martyrs or perfect; include child saints, lay saints, and saints from different cultures. Skip gruesome details with young children. Don’t tell stories as if saints never struggled.',
 'Quick check: ask a child to tell about one saint: name, one thing they did, and one way to copy them this week. A concrete, doable choice shows it landed.',
]),
'2-5-3': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: heaven is real, a joyful life with God, and Jesus is preparing a place for us. This gives hope to children who have faced loss or fear of death.',
 'Heaven is perfect life and love with the Trinity, Mary, the angels and all the blessed (CCC 1023-1024). Jesus says he goes to prepare a place and will take us to himself (John 14:1-3).',
 'Say: “Heaven is being with God and everyone who loves him, with joy that never ends.” To “Will my grandpa be there?”, say: “We hope so and we pray for him. God loves him even more than we do.”',
 'Avoid clouds and harps, and avoid scaring children with hell. If a child has lost someone, follow their lead and be gentle; don’t assure what you can’t know. Tell the family if a child is distressed.',
 'Quick check: ask, “Who is in heaven? What will it be like?” Listen for God and people we love and joy, not cloud details. Then ask what Jesus said in John 14.',
]),
'2-6-1': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: the first commandments tell us how to love God, and they are gifts, not traps. At seven, children often hear “rules” as threats, so show the love underneath.',
 'God gave the Commandments to a people he had already freed; Jesus says they lead to eternal life (CCC 2052). The first three: God alone, his name respected (CCC 2142), the Lord’s Day kept holy (CCC 2168).',
 'Say: “Loving God means putting him first, saying his name with love, and giving him Sunday.” To “Is saying ‘Oh my God’ bad?”, say: “His name is special, so we say it in prayer, not as a joke or when angry.”',
 'Don’t shame children for words they hear at home; don’t make a family feel judged for missing Sunday Mass. Keep the tone invitational. Stress that the God who gives commandments also forgives.',
 'Quick check: ask, “What are three ways to love God first?” Listen for pray, say his name with respect, go to Mass. A child who can say why (“because I love him”) has the heart of it.',
]),
'2-6-2': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: God’s rules protect the people we love: parents, every life, and the truth. This helps seven-year-olds see rules as care for others.',
 'The fourth commandment shows the order of charity, honoring parents who give us life (CCC 2197). Human life is sacred, belonging to God from its beginning to its end (CCC 2258). We must tell the truth (CCC 2464).',
 'Say: “Honor means thanks, help and respect. Respect life means we never hurt others on purpose, and we tell the truth.” To “What if my parent is mean?”, say: “We still treat them with respect, and God wants you safe; tell a trusted adult.”',
 'Many children live in blended, single-parent or difficult homes; say “parents and those who care for you.” Never imply a child must obey abuse. Explain life lessons gently without graphic detail.',
 'Quick check: ask for one way to honor a parent, one way to respect life (kindness, caring for animals and people), and one truth-telling moment. Concrete examples show understanding.',
]),
'2-6-3': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: being thankful for what we have frees us from grabbing and comparing. Seven-year-olds feel envy strongly, so name it kindly.',
 'The seventh commandment forbids unjustly taking another’s goods (CCC 2401). The tenth forbids coveting what belongs to others and is the root of stealing (CCC 2534). A pure heart is also called for (CCC 2514).',
 'Say: “Wanting is okay. Envy is when someone else’s good thing makes us sad or mean. Thanking God for what we have helps.” To “What if I take something by accident?”, say: “Give it back and say sorry; that makes it right.”',
 'Never shame children from low-income families or who have less. Avoid blaming a child who took something. Frame sharing as joy, not loss; let them choose to share when possible.',
 'Quick check: ask, “What can you say or do when you wish you had a friend’s toy?” Listen for thanking God, asking to play together, or being glad for the friend.',
]),
'2-7-1': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: the Our Father is Jesus’ own prayer, and we can pray it with understanding. Children say it by heart, so the goal is to make the words mean something.',
 'Jesus gave the Our Father when his disciples asked him to teach them to pray (CCC 2759). It has seven petitions: the first three focus on God’s glory, the last four bring our needs to the Father (CCC 2761, 2765).',
 'Say: “We tell God he is our Father, ask his will, then ask for bread, forgiveness and protection.” To “Why do we ask for forgiveness as we forgive?”, say: “God’s mercy helps us forgive others too.”',
 'Avoid speed recitation. Watch for children for whom “father” is painful; emphasize that God is a loving Father. Children from other traditions may use different wording; just invite, don’t correct harshly.',
 'Quick check: ask each child to pick one petition and say it in their own words. Another test: pray it slowly together and see who can show the meaning with a gesture.',
]),
'2-7-2': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: Church prayers are treasures we can use anytime, and memorizing them gives us words when we have none. This matters before Reconciliation and Communion, when children need familiar prayers.',
 'The Church hands down set prayers to nourish continual prayer (CCC 2698). The Hail Mary joins the angel’s greeting (Luke 1:28) with a request for help. The Act of Contrition expresses sorrow, trust in God’s mercy, and a firm purpose.',
 'Say: “These are words many saints have prayed, and we can borrow them.” To “Do I have to feel something?”, say: “No, just mean it as best you can; God hears your heart.”',
 'Don’t turn memorization into a test or shame slow readers. Offer cards or pictures. Children with learning differences may need extra time; celebrate trying. Keep the meaning, not just the sound.',
 'Quick check: ask a child to tell you in their own words what one prayer is saying (“Glory Be: praise to God”), then lead it. Understanding, not speed, is the goal.',
]),
'2-7-3': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: God speaks in quiet, and listening is a kind of prayer. Children rarely get quiet, so a few calm seconds can be a gift.',
 'Meditation is a quest, seeking to understand what the Lord asks and respond (CCC 2705). Praying takes a decided will; we make time for the Lord (CCC 2710). Samuel learned to say, “Speak, Lord, your servant is listening.”',
 'Say: “Be still and tell Jesus you’re listening. Maybe you’ll think of something kind to do.” To “Did God talk to you?”, say: “Usually not in a voice; he can speak through thoughts, Scripture and people. Check with a grown-up.”',
 'Don’t force silence or measure “success.” Restless or anxious children, and those who have trauma, may find closing eyes hard; let them keep eyes open. Don’t claim children will hear God audibly.',
 'Quick check: after 30 seconds of quiet, ask, “What did you notice?” Any honest answer, even “I was thinking about lunch,” is fine. Praise the trying and ask how to try again.',
]),
'2-8-1': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: Advent is for getting ready and Christmas is a long celebration of Jesus’ birth. Children are swamped by commercial Christmas, so a Church rhythm gives them a counterweight.',
 'In Advent the Church renews the ancient longing for the Messiah and prepares for his coming (CCC 524). The Christmas season continues after December 25 through the Baptism of the Lord (CCC 1171).',
 'Say: “Advent is getting our hearts ready, like preparing a room for a guest. Christmas is the party, and it lasts many days.” To “Is Santa real?”, say: “Santa is a fun story based on St. Nicholas, and Jesus’ birth is the real reason.”',
 'Be sensitive to families who cannot afford gifts, are grieving, or have divided holidays. Don’t make Advent a performance; small acts suffice. Don’t criticize family traditions.',
 'Quick check: ask, “What do we do in Advent? When does Christmas season end?” Look for prepare, pray, be kind, and “after Jesus’ baptism.” Ask for one kind act planned.',
]),
'2-8-2': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: Lent is a time to get closer to Jesus through prayer, fasting and giving, and Easter is our joy that he is alive. For seven-year-olds, it is about love, not punishment.',
 'Lent is the Church’s season of penance (CCC 538, 1438); Jesus teaches prayer, fasting and almsgiving (Matthew 6). Easter is the Feast of feasts (CCC 1169), and the Easter season lasts fifty days.',
 'Say: “In Lent we make room for Jesus by giving up something small and doing something kind. Easter is the big yes: Jesus is alive!” To “Why did Jesus have to die?”, say: “Because he loves us, and he rose to give us life.”',
 'Do not encourage extreme fasting or food restrictions for children; choose small, kind promises. Be mindful of children with food insecurity or health issues. Avoid fear-heavy Good Friday detail.',
 'Quick check: ask each child to name one prayer, one giving, and one sacrifice promise. Then ask, “What do we celebrate at Easter?” Look for “Jesus is alive,” not just eggs.',
]),
'2-8-3': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: the Church year is full of celebrations that remind us of God’s friends and the saints in heaven. Feast days give seven-year-olds a family-sized Church to belong to.',
 'The Church honors Mary and the saints throughout the year, celebrating the mysteries of Christ (CCC 1172). Sundays and holy days of obligation call us to Mass (CCC 2043). All Saints is November 1; All Souls is November 2.',
 'Say: “Feasts are days when the Church has a party for God’s friends.” To “Why do we pray for dead people on All Souls?”, say: “Love doesn’t stop. We ask God to bring them to heaven.”',
 'Be careful with Halloween: don’t demonize it or families’ choices. Instead, offer saint-costume alternatives. Children who recently lost someone may feel tender on All Souls; be gentle.',
 'Quick check: ask, “What feast is on November 1? Whose party is it?” Look for All Saints and “everyone in heaven.” Ask for one feast day they can celebrate at home.',
]),
'2-L1': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: the quiet voice inside is a gift from God that helps you choose well, and you can make it stronger. Seven-year-olds are choosing on their own and need practical tools.',
 'Conscience is a judgment of reason about right and wrong, and in it we hear God’s call to do good (CCC 1776, 1778). It must be formed by God’s Word, prayer and advice (CCC 1783, 1785).',
 'Say: “It’s like a little compass that points to what’s good. You train it by listening, praying, and asking.” To “What if I don’t listen?”, say: “You may feel uneasy; say sorry, start again, and go to Confession.”',
 'Watch for scrupulous or anxious children: a guilt feeling is not always sin. Distinguish a guiding voice from a nagging one and encourage talking to a parent or priest. Don’t use conscience to scare.',
 'Quick check: ask for a time they felt that quiet voice and what they did. Then ask, “How can you help it grow?” Look for listening, praying, learning, asking.',
]),
'2-L2': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: prayer is talking and listening to God, anytime and anywhere, in short words as well as set ones. This frees children from thinking prayer is only for church or bedtime.',
 'Prayer is raising the mind and heart to God (CCC 2559). Jesus taught his disciples to pray (CCC 2759), and the Church’s prayers and rhythms help us pray through the day (CCC 2698). Paul says to pray without ceasing.',
 'Say: “Prayer is talking with your best friend, who is always there. You can say thank you, sorry, please help, or I love you.” To “Does God hear me if I forget?”, say: “Yes, he is always listening; just start again.”',
 'Avoid prayer as a performance or a “magic” request. Be gentle with children whose prayers weren’t answered, such as for a sick relative: God hears, and he is with us, even when things don’t change.',
 'Quick check: ask, “Name a place you could pray this week and what you’d say.” Look for varied places and a short real prayer. A child who offers a spontaneous one has understood.',
]),
'2-L3': dict(teach=[
 'Get across: Sunday is the Lord’s Day, a gift of Mass, rest and family joy. Many children have crowded schedules, so naming Sunday’s purpose gives them a rhythm.',
 'Sunday is the day of Christ’s Resurrection (CCC 2174), the day the Church gathers for the Eucharist, the source and summit of Christian life (CCC 1324). Rest and leisure for family are part of the Lord’s Day (CCC 2184-2186).',
 'Say: “God rested, so we rest too. On Sunday we go see Jesus at Mass, then enjoy family and play.” To “Why is Mass boring?”, say: “Sometimes it feels long. Look for what Jesus does, and tell him you’re there for him.”',
 'Many families work Sundays or have custody schedules; don’t make children feel guilty. Don’t treat rest as laziness or Sunday as only rules. Invite, not pressure, and share what is possible.',
 'Quick check: ask, “What are three things we do on Sunday?” Look for Mass, rest and play, family. Then ask, “What is one way to make Sunday special at your home?”',
]),
}
